67\">+=</span><span style=\"--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#ADBAC7\"> dy;</span></span>\n<span data-line=\"\"><span style=\"--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#ADBAC7\">        }</span></span>\n<span data-line=\"\"><span style=\"--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#ADBAC7\">    }</span></span>\n<span data-line=\"\"><span style=\"--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#ADBAC7\">}</span></span></code></pre></figure>\n<p>The important design decision is that <code>Scene</code> <strong>owns</strong> the document and lives\nacross calls. Rebuilding the whole document on every pointer move is the mistake\nthat makes wasm feel slower than JS.</p>\n<h2 id=\"the-boundary-rules\">The boundary rules</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Rule</th>\n<th>Why</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Pass numbers, not objects</td>\n<td>Every <code>JsValue</code> conversion copies</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Keep state in Rust</td>\n<td>A handle (<code>u32</code>) beats a 2 MB round trip</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Batch calls</td>\n<td>1 call per frame, not 1 per node</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Use <code>Float32Array</code> views for bulk data</td>\n<td>Zero-copy over shared linear memory</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Measure bundle size in CI</td>\n<td>It creeps by 30 KB per dependency</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<figure data-rehype-pretty-code-figure=\"\"><pre tabindex=\"0\" data-language=\"js\" data-theme=\"github-light github-dark-dimmed\"><code data-language=\"js\" data-theme=\"github-light github-dark-dimmed\" style=\"display: grid;\"><span data-line=\"\"><span style=\"--shiki-light:#6A737D;--shiki-dark:#768390\">// one call per frame, not per node</span></span>\n<span data-line=\"\"><span style=\"--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F47067\">const</span><span style=\"--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#6CB6FF\"> rects</span><span style=\"--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F47067\"> =</span><span style=\"--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#ADBAC7\"> scene.</span><span style=\"--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#DCBDFB\">layout</span><span style=\"--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#ADBAC7\">();          </span><span style=\"--shiki-light:#6A737D;--shiki-dark:#768390\">// returns a Float32Array view</span></span>\n<span data-line=\"\"><span style=\"--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#ADBAC7\">ctx.</span><span style=\"--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#DCBDFB\">putImageData</span><span style=\"--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#ADBAC7\">(</span><span style=\"--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F47067\">new</span><span style=\"--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#DCBDFB\"> ImageData</span><span style=\"--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#ADBAC7\">(</span><span style=\"--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F47067\">new</span><span style=\"--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#DCBDFB\"> Uint8ClampedArray</span><span style=\"--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#ADBAC7\">(rects), w, h), </span><span style=\"--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#6CB6FF\">0</span><span style=\"--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#ADBAC7\">, </span><span style=\"--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#6CB6FF\">0</span><span style=\"--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#ADBAC7\">);</span></span></code></pre></figure>\n<h2 id=\"when-to-reach-for-wasm-and-when-not-to\">When to reach for wasm, and when not to</h2>\n<p><strong>Reach for it when:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>You are doing per-frame numeric work (layout, physics, image, audio, crypto).</li>\n<li>You have an existing Rust library you want in the browser (parsers, codecs,\ncompression, simulation).</li>\n<li>The same logic must run in the browser, at the edge and on the server, and\ndivergence would be a bug.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Do not reach for it when:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>The work is DOM manipulation. JS is faster at that, because it is native to it.</li>\n<li>You need a form. A 200 KB wasm bundle to validate an email address is a\nself-inflicted wound.</li>\n<li>Your team does not already know Rust — the iteration cost is real.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"what-to-steal-for-your-own-project\">What to steal for your own project</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Start with one function</strong>, not a framework. <code>resize(imageBytes)</code> that beats\nthe JS version is a complete win; a rewritten app is a project.</li>\n<li><strong>Budget the bytes.</strong> <code>wasm-opt -Oz</code> plus brotli turns a 1 MB module into ~200 KB.\nPut the number in CI so it cannot regress.</li>\n<li><strong>Keep the UI in the UI layer.</strong> Figma did not rewrite its interface in Rust;\nit rewrote the engine.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"try-it\">Try it</h2>\n<p>Take a hot loop from any web app — sorting 100k rows, parsing a large CSV,\ncomputing a diff — and implement it twice: once in JS, once in Rust/wasm. Compare\n<code>performance.now()</code> around each, and the bundle delta. If the win is under 3×, keep\nthe JS.</p>\n<p><strong>Back to:</strong> <a href=\"../README.md\">Use cases index</a></p>"}